1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,759
Every service operation runs on updates from many different sources.

2
00:00:03,979 --> 00:00:08,896
The challenge is moving that information accurately, and finding it again when it matters.

3
00:00:09,716 --> 00:00:15,650
When that fails, time is lost: people ask again, search, and re-key the same update.

4
00:00:15,870 --> 00:00:21,532
Resources get over-committed: a job is double-booked, or the same part is ordered twice.

5
00:00:21,752 --> 00:00:27,970
It need not be. Feed updates in easily, from any operation, and the information lands in one place.

6
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:33,105
Then supervisors can look up the state of operations, ask a question, and get an answer.

7
00:00:33,825 --> 00:00:39,022
Our answer: the Service Operations Agent, powered by Deloitte and NVIDIA.

8
00:00:39,742 --> 00:00:44,895
One way to feed in updates for any kind of service operation, shown here in automotive.

9
00:00:45,615 --> 00:00:48,444
Everyone sees the same live state of operations.

10
00:00:48,664 --> 00:00:51,300
Every job, with the reason it needs attention.

11
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:54,582
Like the 17 vehicles flagged unsafe to release.

12
00:00:55,602 --> 00:01:00,012
Updates go in once, by voice or text, in the technician's own words.

13
00:01:00,232 --> 00:01:04,452
NVIDIA Riva transcribes it, and Nemotron extracts the facts.

14
00:01:04,672 --> 00:01:08,757
Code checks every fact against the record, and flags contradictions.

15
00:01:08,977 --> 00:01:11,016
Each update joins an audit trail.

16
00:01:11,236 --> 00:01:14,485
Supervisors simply ask, and receive cited answers.

17
00:01:15,505 --> 00:01:18,983
Follow one update. A technician dictates it from the bay.

18
00:01:19,203 --> 00:01:21,842
NVIDIA Riva turns the speech into text.

19
00:01:22,062 --> 00:01:26,052
Nemotron extracts the facts: the measurement and the spec.

20
00:01:26,872 --> 00:01:31,604
Code verifies it and flags the repair order unsafe to release, instantly.

21
00:01:32,924 --> 00:01:37,751
A supervisor asks: which vehicles cannot be released on safety grounds?

22
00:01:37,971 --> 00:01:41,962
18 vehicles, each one cited, in a tenth of a second.

23
00:01:42,582 --> 00:01:46,797
Another question: which parts are holding up more than one job?

24
00:01:47,017 --> 00:01:50,544
Order once, and clear several jobs, in a tenth of a second.

25
00:01:51,264 --> 00:01:55,620
An open question: has anyone seen a whistling noise on a Passat?

26
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:00,657
Search, reranking and a cited answer, in seconds, on one GPU.

27
00:02:01,877 --> 00:02:07,447
Behind it, the architecture: three NVIDIA NIMs on one L40S.

28
00:02:08,067 --> 00:02:12,521
Riva handles speech. NeMo Guardrails stops unsafe requests.

29
00:02:12,741 --> 00:02:16,357
NeMo Evaluator scores every release: the whole stack.

30
00:02:17,577 --> 00:02:22,682
It is all observable: DCGM and app metrics, live in Grafana.

31
00:02:23,702 --> 00:02:27,969
Business impact: unsafe vehicles surface the moment they are logged.

32
00:02:28,189 --> 00:02:30,922
Supervisors get answers in a tenth of a second.

33
00:02:31,142 --> 00:02:35,596
Every figure traces to a record, and one update feeds the whole operation.

34
00:02:36,316 --> 00:02:41,050
In summary: updates go in once, and everyone sees the same state.

35
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:45,904
Answers in a tenth of a second, traced to records, on one GPU you own.

36
00:02:46,124 --> 00:02:48,626
Fast. Grounded. Yours to run.

